  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1: Nor'easter filling as its center pushes inland across \nSouthern New Jersey.\n\nThe well-defined center of the nor-easter is right over KACY and\nwill push inland a bit more before becoming nearly stationary\nover Southern NJ or Delaware Bay late today/tonight. The center\nof the low will drift slowly offshore again Monday and track \nsouth of and parallel with the southern shore of Long Island. \n\nA few distinct vort lobes were pivoting to the west this morning \nwith one band of light to briefly mdt rain moving over the\nCentral/Scent mountains bringing a few to several hundredths \nof an inch of rain. A second and slightly more robust N/S \nrain band drifting wwd across the Susq Valley could be\naccompanied by 1-2 tenths of an inch.\n\nPrevious...\n\nThe wind and gusts will start to decrease a bit through the \ndaylight hours, and will do so even more tonight. The loosening \npressure gradient will mean that the threat for nearing wind \nadvisory criteria over any of our counties is all but over. We \ndid knock a couple degs off the dewpoints for today as the NBM \nhas been consistently too high over the past 2-3 days. Despite \nthis, the bands of rain should go farther to the west than Sat -\nso we bumped the PoPs to categorical for most of the CWA east \nof route 219. The rain may not quite reach the ground in the \nwestern layer of zones, but some hit the ground locally despite \nthe lower-than-forecast dewpoints on Sat. Cool air is locked in,\nand maxes will be only 55-65F - coolest NE.\n\nQPF is looking heaviest just about where it was on Saturday -\nthe Lower Susq. Someone might pick up another inch, but most\nlikely not. Even so, the FFG is high enough to not have flooding\neven be a thought. \n\nConditions will improve tonight and Monday morning. By noon\nMonday, there should only be isolated --SHRA over the NE and the\nwind will not be notable. Temps rise slightly from Sun, but will\nstill be cooler than normal. \n\n  -----------------------------------------------------------\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2: Tranquil conditions expected for the first half \nof next week with a slight warming trend emerging across the \nregion.\n\nDry conditions will continue into Thursday as the front\nanticipated to be arriving late Wed/night will be delayed for a\nday or so. It will pick up some moisture from the remnants of\nPolo. But, that precip probably stays to our W until Thursday. All\nmodel guidance trends towards a warming trend in this \ntimeframe, with above-average temperatures prevailing area wide\nfor the balance of the week. Someone may hit 80F before Friday.\nDetails on timing and intensity of rainfall are not easy to pin\ndown at this range and with the upcoming pattern. So, we'll just\nmake a gradual increase in PoPs Fri-Sat-Sun.",
